        "SIGNALMENT": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cat and dog.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Small-breed dogs may be predisposed; animals with ductal plate malformations (DPM) predisposed: particularly Caroli DPM phenotypes (i.e., malformative dilated large ducts causing bile stasis in sacculated ducts); microhepatolithiasis encountered more often in DPM.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hyperlipidemic and hypercholesterolemic dogs—predisposed to GBM; may relate to endocrine disorders, idiopathic condition, or other disorders (see Gallbladder Mucocele).",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SIGNS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNS",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "May be asymptomatic.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "When accompanied by infection or causing intermittent or complete EHBDO (with or without peritonitis)—vomiting; meal-related discomfort, vague abdominal pain; fever; ± jaundice.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Episodic vague peri- or postprandial abdominal pain or behavior seeking position of relief.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "CAUSES": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS",
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Predisposing factors—stasis of bile flow: GB dysmotility, choledochal cysts (cat especially, form of DPM); lith nidus may evolve from inflammatory debris, infection, epithelial irregularity/exfoliation, neoplasia, residual suture material; bile supersaturation: heme-bilirubin pigments, hemobilia, calcium-bilirubinate, enhanced mucin production (inflammation, prostaglandins), cholesterol.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Fused feline pancreatic and bile duct predisposes to concurrent biliary/pancreatic cholelithiasis, choledochitis, and bile stasis progressing to EHBDO as well as cholelithinitiated pancreatitis or signs mistaken for pancreatitis.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Bile sludge associated with GB distention— may enhance mucin production and coalescence of bile particulates increasing risk for choleliths; but EHBDO does not cause choleliths.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Inflammatory mediators and bacterial enzymes associated with cholecystitis— increase risk for stone precipitation.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hemobilia—bleeding into GB or bile ducts or chronic hemolysis can lead to hemeinitiated cholelith formation.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Low-methionine and high-cholesterol experimental diet in dogs is proven lithogenic.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    "DIAGNOSIS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "EHBDO—inflammatory, infectious, or neoplastic conditions involving liver, CBD, or extrahepatic tissues adjacent to porta hepatis; suggested by marked increases in alkaline phosphatase (ALP),γ-glutamyltransferase (GGT), bilirubin, and gradual increase in cholesterol.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cholangiohepatitis.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cholecystitis/choledochitis.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Bile peritonitis.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "GBM.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Consequence of DPM.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Chronic hypercalcemia may increase risk.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "May have no clinicopathologic abnormalities—asymptomatic cholelithiasis.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "CBC—may be normal; may reflect bacterial infection, endotoxemia, biliary obstruction, or other underlying causal factors; inflammatory leukogram in some cases.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Biochemistry—if symptomatic: variable hyperbilirubinemia, increase ALP, GGT, alanine aminotransferase (ALT), and aspartate aminotransferase (AST) activities; also see Bile Duct Obstruction (Extrahepatic).",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "CBC/CHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "CBC/CHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "OTHER LABORATORY TESTS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OTHER LABORATORY TESTS",
          "canonical_heading": "OTHER LABORATORY TESTS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Bacterial culture—bile: aerobic and anaerobic bacteria often confirmed in symptomatic patients if crushed cholelith, sludged bile, ± GB wall (if cholecystectomy) submitted for cultures.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cholelith nidus—may house associated bacterial infection.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Coagulation profile—bleeding associated with prolonged clotting times may develop with EHBDO of >7–10 days; see Bile Duct Obstruction (Extrahepatic); responsive to parenteral vitamin K1administration.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cholelith analysis—infrequently done; submit to laboratory equipped for cholelith analyses.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "IMAGING": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "IMAGING",
          "canonical_heading": "IMAGING",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Abdominal radiography—limited value in delineating GB structure/content; choleliths often small, may be radiolucent; rarely mistaken for dystrophic biliary mineralization in animals with chronic cholangitis or Caroli DPM phenotype.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Ultrasonography—can detect: choleliths ≥2 mm diameter, thickened GB wall, distended biliary tract, increased hepatic parenchymal echogenicity and extrahepatic ductal involvement; may facilitate specimen collection for culture, cytology, and histopathology; may detect evidence of EHBDO",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "within 72h;caution: distended GB with bile",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "“sludge” is common in anorectic or fasted",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 3,
              "subheading": "within 72h;caution: distended GB with bile"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "patients; do not mistake for GB obstruction.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 3,
              "subheading": "within 72h;caution: distended GB with bile"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hepatolithiasis usually casts acoustic shadows.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 3,
              "subheading": "within 72h;caution: distended GB with bile"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Imaging of choleliths within extrahepatic",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 3,
              "subheading": "within 72h;caution: distended GB with bile"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "ducts may be difficult owing to enteric gas",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 3,
              "subheading": "within 72h;caution: distended GB with bile"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "obstructing imaging “window”; confirmation",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 3,
              "subheading": "within 72h;caution: distended GB with bile"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "of distended intrahepatic bile duct done with",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 3,
              "subheading": "within 72h;caution: distended GB with bile"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "color-flow Doppler.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 3,
              "subheading": "within 72h;caution: distended GB with bile"
            }
          ]
        },
        "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES",
          "canonical_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Histopathologic evaluation of liver necessary in patients undergoing surgical cholelith removal to detect comorbid conditions influencing treatment and prognosis, i.e., suppurative cholangitis, nonsuppurative cholangitis, DPM, neoplasia.",
              "pdf_page": 349,
              "printed_page": 267,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "DIAGNOSTIC TESTS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC TESTS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS",
          "content_blocks": []
        }
      }
    },
    "TREATMENT": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Controversial whether choleresis with ursodeoxycholate (UDCA) is beneficial in animals lacking clinical or clinicopathologic signs; can resolve cholesterol choleliths in humans, but these are rare in dogs or cats; choleresis with UDCA may help move sludged biliary debris.",
          "pdf_page": 349,
          "printed_page": 267,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Supportive fluids—if hospitalized, according to hydration, electrolyte, and acid-base status.",
          "pdf_page": 349,
          "printed_page": 267,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "If hyperlipidemia coexistent—prescribe fat-restricted diet, identify and manage endocrinopathies; if failure to control hyperlipidemia, may require additional medical management.",
          "pdf_page": 349,
          "printed_page": 267,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Control predisposing conditions, especially biliary tree infection (with antimicrobials) and GB dysmotility (by GB removal).",
          "pdf_page": 349,
          "printed_page": 267,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Exploratory surgery, choledochotomy, cholecystotomy, and possibly cholecystectomy or biliary-enteric anastomosis—indicated in symptomatic cases according to circumstances.",
          "pdf_page": 349,
          "printed_page": 267,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Warn client that cholelithiasis is chronic problem and that stones may reform even after surgical removal despite chronic medical treatment; choleliths in GB is strong",
          "pdf_page": 349,
          "printed_page": 267,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "indication for cholecystectomy unless GB needed for biliary enteric anastomosis in certain conditions.",
          "pdf_page": 350,
          "printed_page": 268,
          "column": 1
        }
      ],
